# name: test/sql/crypta/crypta_flush_unconfigured_refusal.test
# description: The SECOND decode site - the inlined-deletion flush path - also refuses a wrapped key
# group: [crypta]
#
# PRIVATE-FORK ONLY. Never cherry-pick to the public upstream fork.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE PROVES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT
# --------------------------------------------------------
# It proves the GUARD at src/functions/ducklake_flush_inlined_data.cpp FIRES, and
# that it does not fire on a plaintext key. It does NOT prove the shape ARISES
# naturally from HEAD, because since #25 it cannot.
#
# That distinction is the whole reason this file is written the way it is, so it
# is stated first rather than buried:
#
#   - The guard's ENTIRE input is the stored `encryption_key` STRING and the
#     absence of a crypta provider. `DuckLakeCatalog::RefuseWrappedKeyWithoutCrypta`
#     calls `CryptaClient::LooksWrapped(stored_key)` and nothing else; it never
#     decodes, never decrypts, and never reaches a socket. A planted
#     wrapped-LOOKING value therefore drives the predicate COMPLETELY and
#     faithfully - there is no part of the guard a "real" blob would exercise
#     that this does not.
#   - The lake this fixture builds is a LEGACY lake: one that inlined deletions
#     while it had no crypta, and whose delete-file key later became a wrapped
#     blob. That is a real shape - every lake that inlined before #25 landed is
#     one - and it is the shape the guard exists for.
#   - It is NOT constructible from current code. #25 forbids inlining on a crypta
#     lake at ATTACH, and `DuckLakeCatalog::DataInliningRowLimit` returns 0
#     whenever `crypta_provider` is set, ahead of every option scope and of the
#     global setting. So a configured crypta lake can never inline, the
#     unconfigured session cannot inline either (any DELETE would have to scan a
#     wrapped data file, which refuses at `ReadDataFile`), and no sequence of
#     public operations reaches this state. Recorded at #41.
#
# So: the key is PLANTED, on purpose, and this file does not pretend otherwise.
# An earlier version built the shape by attaching a crypta lake with
# DATA_INLINING_ROW_LIMIT 100; that ATTACH is now refused outright, which is what
# forced the rewrite.
#
# WHY THE SITE NEEDS ITS OWN FILE AT ALL
# ---------------------------------------
# crypta_unconfigured_reader_refusal.test covers the unwrap choke point in
# DuckLakeMetadataManager::ReadDataFile. That is NOT the only place a stored
# encryption key is base64-decoded and handed to the Parquet reader.
#
# ducklake_flush_inlined_data.cpp reads `existing_del.encryption_key` straight out
# of ducklake_delete_file with its own hand-written SQL query, decodes it with
# Blob::FromBase64, and passes it to DuckLakeDeleteFilter::ScanDeleteFile. It never
# goes near ReadDataFile, so the refusal there does not cover it. An unconfigured
# reader that flushes inlined deletions over an existing delete file got the same
# INTERNAL assertion failure #20 was filed about:
#
#   INTERNAL Error: Invalid AES key length for GCM
#
# The original fix's comment called ReadDataFile "THE unwrap choke point - every
# read of every encrypted file funnels through here". That was false, an
# adversarial review caught it, and this site is the correction. It is also the
# ONLY test of that call site: `grep -rn RefuseWrappedKeyWithoutCrypta test/`
# finds nothing else, in C++ or SQL.
#
# NO KEY SERVICE, AND THEREFORE NO require-env
# ---------------------------------------------
# Nothing here attaches crypta, so nothing needs the fake. The `require-env` this
# file used to carry is gone deliberately: it was true of the old fixture and is a
# false dependency for this one. `run_sql_crypta_tests.sh` DERIVES its skip-guard
# list with `grep -l '^require-env'`, so the guard follows automatically rather
# than needing an edit. The file now also runs under a plain
# `unittest "test/sql/crypta/*"` in the generic jobs, which is strictly more
# coverage than before.
#
# NOTE on the CONFIGURED direction, which is a different defect: this same flush
# path never calls UnwrapKey, and WriteNewDeleteFiles DOES wrap delete-file keys,
# so a CONFIGURED crypta lake is broken here too. That is pre-existing, has its
# own issue (#26), and is deliberately NOT what this file tests.

require ducklake

require parquet

require httpfs

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Fixture: a plain-ENCRYPTED lake with inlining, carrying a delete FILE and
# further deletions still inlined and unflushed
#
# No crypta anywhere in this phase - that is what makes it constructible. The
# lake is ENCRYPTED, so DuckLake mints a per-file key and stores it base64 in the
# catalog; with no crypta configured that key is PLAINTEXT, which is exactly the
# pre-envelope state a legacy lake is in.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

statement ok
ATTACH 'ducklake:__TEST_DIR__/crypta_flush.db' AS seed (DATA_PATH '__TEST_DIR__/crypta_flush_files', ENCRYPTED, DATA_INLINING_ROW_LIMIT 100, METADATA_CATALOG 'seed_meta')

statement ok
CREATE TABLE seed.alpha AS SELECT i AS id FROM range(50) t(i)

# Push the inlined rows out into a real Parquet data file, so the deletions below
# are deletions OF A FILE rather than of inlined rows.
statement ok
CALL ducklake_flush_inlined_data('seed')

statement ok
DELETE FROM seed.alpha WHERE id < 5

# This flush is what creates the delete FILE, with its own key.
statement ok
CALL ducklake_flush_inlined_data('seed')

# THE INSTRUMENT, SHOWN LIVE AND BEFORE ANY PLANTING. Exactly one delete file, and
# its key is the 44-character base64 of a raw 32-byte DEK - the plaintext shape.
# Asserting the length here is what makes the planted value later a CHANGE rather
# than a value of unknown provenance, and it is also why the over-refusal control
# below means anything: 44 is exactly the floor `CryptaClient::LooksWrapped` uses,
# so this real key sits on the boundary the guard must not trip on.
query II
SELECT count(*), count(*) FILTER (WHERE length(encryption_key) = 44) FROM seed_meta.ducklake_delete_file
----
1	1

# And it does NOT look wrapped, which is the state the refusals below have to be a
# change FROM. A fixture whose "before" already satisfied the guard would prove
# nothing about the guard.
query I
SELECT bool_and(NOT starts_with(encryption_key, 'RExL')) FROM seed_meta.ducklake_delete_file
----
true

# Keep the REAL key. The control at the end restores it and requires the flush to
# SUCCEED, which only works if the key is the true one - so this table is
# load-bearing, not bookkeeping.
statement ok
CREATE TABLE real_delete_key AS SELECT encryption_key AS k FROM seed_meta.ducklake_delete_file

# More deletions, left INLINED - not flushed. These are what a later reader will
# try to flush, forcing it to read the existing delete file above.
statement ok
DELETE FROM seed.alpha WHERE id > 45

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Plant the wrapped-looking key - the one step current code cannot produce
#
# 'RExL' plus 220 base64 characters: past the 44-character floor, carrying the
# magic, and inside the 208-280 range a real blob occupies. Deliberately NOT a
# decodable blob for anything - the guard never decodes, so a decodable one would
# test nothing more, and a value that could be mistaken for a genuine round-trip
# would misrepresent what this file proves.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

statement ok
UPDATE seed_meta.ducklake_delete_file SET encryption_key = 'RExL' || repeat('A', 220)

# The plant took, and took the shape the guard keys on - both halves of
# LooksWrapped's predicate, asserted separately so a wrong one names itself.
query III
SELECT count(*), count(*) FILTER (WHERE starts_with(encryption_key, 'RExL')), count(*) FILTER (WHERE length(encryption_key) > 44) FROM seed_meta.ducklake_delete_file
----
1	1	1

statement ok
DETACH seed

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CASE: re-attach WITHOUT the crypta options and flush
#
# No DELETE is issued here on purpose. The deletions are already inlined from the
# seed session; the flush alone drives the second decode site, and adding a DELETE
# would pull the data-file read path into a file that is not about it.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

statement ok
ATTACH 'ducklake:__TEST_DIR__/crypta_flush.db' AS unconfigured (DATA_PATH '__TEST_DIR__/crypta_flush_files', DATA_INLINING_ROW_LIMIT 100, METADATA_CATALOG 'unconf_meta')

statement error
CALL ducklake_flush_inlined_data('unconfigured')
----
carries a crypta-wrapped encryption key

statement error
CALL ducklake_flush_inlined_data('unconfigured')
----
CRYPTA_SOCKET

statement error
CALL ducklake_flush_inlined_data('unconfigured')
----
Re-attach with the crypta options

statement ok
DETACH unconfigured

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONTROL - the guard's OWN PREDICATE, in the direction that proves it is not
# simply refusing everything
#
# This REPLACES the old closing control ("the same lake WITH the crypta options
# still attaches and reads"), which cannot survive a planted key: a value that is
# not a real blob would be sent to crypta and rejected, so that control would now
# pass for the wrong reason - the strongest kind of silent rot.
#
# What replaces it is stronger, because it exercises the GUARD rather than the
# lake: the SAME flush, on the SAME lake, through the SAME call site, with a key
# that does NOT look wrapped - and it must PASS THROUGH. That is the positive
# control for `LooksWrapped`'s negative answer, OBSERVED in this run rather than
# assumed.
#
# It is simultaneously the OVER-REFUSAL assertion. The restored key is 44
# characters - exactly `MAX_PLAINTEXT_KEY_BASE64` - so this is the boundary case a
# too-greedy floor breaks: refuse at 44 and every plain-ENCRYPTED lake with no
# crypta becomes unreadable forever, with advice that does not apply. A guard that
# refused everything would pass every assertion above this one and fail here.
#
# The sharper false-positive shape - a plaintext DEK of exactly 44 characters that
# HAPPENS to start with `RExL`, ~6e-8 per file - cannot be built here, because the
# key must be the lake's real one for the flush to succeed and nothing can force a
# minted DEK's leading bytes. It is pinned in C++ instead, at BOTH callers of the
# floor: crypta_refusal_test.cpp ("LooksWrapped does not misread a plaintext DEK
# that happens to start with the magic", which also asserts that one character
# PAST the floor is still admitted) and crypta_cache_test.cpp ("a 44-character
# plaintext DEK that happens to carry the magic"), with the
# `no_plaintext_length_floor` mutant reddening both.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Restored under its OWN attach, which is then DETACHED before the flush runs.
# Every metadata mutation in this file is followed by a fresh ATTACH for exactly
# this reason: DuckLake reads delete-file metadata through the attached catalog,
# so mutating it underneath a live attach would leave it open whether the flush
# saw the new value or a cached one - and a control whose input is uncertain is
# not a control. The refusal phase above already relies on the same discipline.
statement ok
ATTACH 'ducklake:__TEST_DIR__/crypta_flush.db' AS restorer (DATA_PATH '__TEST_DIR__/crypta_flush_files', DATA_INLINING_ROW_LIMIT 100, METADATA_CATALOG 'restorer_meta')

statement ok
UPDATE restorer_meta.ducklake_delete_file SET encryption_key = (SELECT k FROM real_delete_key)

# Back to the plaintext shape, and back below the floor.
query II
SELECT count(*) FILTER (WHERE length(encryption_key) = 44), count(*) FILTER (WHERE starts_with(encryption_key, 'RExL')) FROM restorer_meta.ducklake_delete_file
----
1	0

statement ok
DETACH restorer

statement ok
ATTACH 'ducklake:__TEST_DIR__/crypta_flush.db' AS restored (DATA_PATH '__TEST_DIR__/crypta_flush_files', DATA_INLINING_ROW_LIMIT 100, METADATA_CATALOG 'restored_meta')

# THE CONTROL ITSELF. Same call, same site, no refusal - the guard reads the key,
# answers "not wrapped", and the flush completes.
statement ok
CALL ducklake_flush_inlined_data('restored')

# And it completed CORRECTLY, not merely without throwing: the previously-inlined
# deletions are now applied, and the row set is right. 50 rows minus ids 0-4 minus
# ids 46-49 = 41 rows, sum 1225 - 10 - 190 = 1025. A flush that silently dropped
# or mis-applied the deletions lands here.
query II
SELECT count(*), sum(id) FROM restored.alpha
----
41	1025

statement ok
DETACH restored
